[
  {
    "name": "Welcome to GenomeSpy",
    "notes": "This tour demonstrates [GenomeSpy](https://genomespy.app/) with data from the Mouse Cancer Cell Line Atlas (MCCA), a non-human cancer model resource described by Mueller et al. in [*A disease model resource reveals core principles of tissue-specific cancer evolution*](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-026-10187-2).\n\nThis visualization is an independent GenomeSpy demo and is not affiliated with or endorsed by the MCCA publication authors. The visualization specs, reproducible workflow, provenance, and rights review are available in the [MCCA dataset recipe](https://github.com/genome-spy/genomespy-dataset-recipes/tree/main/recipes/mcca-cell-line-atlas-mm10).\n\n![Encoding](tour/encoding.svg)\n\nThis visualization displays MCCA cell line annotations together with segmented *copy-ratio* data, somatic short variants, model alleles parsed from the mouse model descriptions, sequencing availability, and selected transcriptome metadata. Segment colors encode log2 copy-ratio values from the source data. Points represent somatic short variants; their size encodes tumor allele fraction, and color encodes variant consequence. A tooltip reveals the exact values of variables in the underlying dataset. Try moving your mouse cursor over the data.",
    "actions": []
  },
  {
    "name": "Navigating around the genome",
    "notes": "You can navigate along the mm10 genome by dragging or by using a touchpad. To zoom, use the mouse wheel or scroll the touchpad up/down using two fingers.\n\n![Using mouse or touchpad](tour/scroll-and-zoom.svg)\n\nThe genome axis uses canonical mm10 chromosomes. The MCCA wrangling excludes alternative contigs, decoys, and other non-canonical sequence names so that copy-ratio segments, mutations, cytobands, and GENCODE M25 gene annotations share the same genomic coordinate system.",
    "actions": []
  },
  {
    "name": "Copy-number patterns",
    "actions": [
      {
        "type": "sampleView/groupByNominal",
        "payload": {
          "attribute": {
            "type": "SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE",
            "specifier": "Annotations/CancerType"
          }
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "sampleView/retainGroupsBySize",
        "payload": {
          "level": 1,
          "measure": "size",
          "operator": "gte",
          "operand": 20
        }
      }
    ],
    "scaleDomains": {
      "x_at_root": [
        {
          "chrom": "chr1",
          "pos": 0
        },
        {
          "chrom": "chrM",
          "pos": 16298
        }
      ]
    },
    "viewSettings": {
      "visibilities": [
        {
          "scope": ["copy-ratios"],
          "view": "copy-ratio-g-score-summary",
          "visible": true
        },
        {
          "scope": [],
          "view": "sample-labels",
          "visible": false
        }
      ]
    },
    "notes": "This bookmark shows exploratory copy-number patterns across *CancerType* groups with at least 20 samples.\n\nSome observations visible in this view:\n- Chromosome 4 loss is prominent in lung, pancreatic, and gastric cancer.\n- Focal Cdkn2a-region losses are apparent in intestinal, lung, and pancreatic cancer.\n- Sarcoma and intestinal cancer show frequent whole-chromosome 6 gain.\n- Focal Myc amplification is visible in many sarcoma samples and is also seen in pancreatic cancer.\n\nCaveat: samples within a *CancerType* are not always independent. Some MCCA lines are matched or related samples from the same mouse, tumor, lesion series, or primary-metastasis case. This view is intended to illustrate copy-number patterns and GenomeSpy workflows, not to estimate recurrence frequencies from a strictly independent cohort."
  },
  {
    "name": "Cdkn2a losses in Kras models",
