  {
    "name": "WES vs lcWGS",
    "title": "Copy-number profiles by sequencing availability",
    "actions": [
      {
        "type": "sampleView/groupCustomCategories",
        "payload": {
          "attribute": {
            "type": "SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE",
            "specifier": "Sequencing/SequencingAvailability"
          },
          "groups": {
            "WES": ["WES only", "lcWGS+WES"],
            "lcWGS": ["lcWGS only"]
          }
        }
      }
    ],
    "scaleDomains": {
      "x_at_root": [
        {
          "chrom": "chr2",
          "pos": 71595392
        },
        {
          "chrom": "chr9",
          "pos": 26136540
        }
      ]
    },
    "viewSettings": {
      "visibilities": [
        {
          "scope": ["mutations"],
          "view": "mutations",
          "visible": false
        },
        {
          "scope": ["copy-ratios"],
          "view": "copy-ratio-g-score-summary",
          "visible": true
        },
        {
          "scope": [],
          "view": "attributeGroup-Annotations",
          "visible": false
        },
        {
          "scope": [],
          "view": "sample-labels",
          "visible": false
        }
      ]
    },
    "notes": "This bookmark groups samples by *SequencingAvailability* to compare copy-number profiles from samples with WES data and samples with lcWGS data only. Samples annotated as \"WES only\" and \"lcWGS+WES\" are grouped together because the MCCA copy-number matrix appears to follow the WES-derived profile when both sequencing types are available. Somatic mutations are hidden here because they are only available for WES samples.\n\nThe difference is visually strong in this window: WES-grouped samples show more narrow, focal-looking copy-ratio variation, whereas the lcWGS-only group has smoother segment patterns at the same scale. This makes *SequencingAvailability* useful context when interpreting apparent focal copy-number events.\n\nThe point is assay provenance, not quality control of individual calls. Narrow, sample-specific copy-ratio changes should be interpreted cautiously, especially in views that mix WES-derived and lcWGS-derived profiles."
  }
